    {
      "evidence_id": "tei:abstract",
      "source": "grobid_tei",
      "field": "abstract",
      "raw_text": "We present PicoGRAM, a practical garbled RAM (GRAM) scheme that achieves a per-instruction communication cost of O(λ • (W • log N • ω(1) + log 3 N)) bits, where N is the RAM space, W is the word width, λ is the computational security parameter, and ω(1) is an arbitrarily small super-constant factor in N. PicoGRAM outperforms previous state-of-the-art GRAMs both asymptotically and concretely. Our key contribution is a novel garbling scheme based on the Decisional Diffie-Hellman (DDH) assumption, which enables efficient Single-Instruction Multiple-Data (SIMD) operations. We implemented PicoGRAM in C++ and evaluated its performance. For N ranging from 2 10 to 2 24 , PicoGRAM reduces communication costs by 5.3× to 12.6× compared to the tri-state GRAM by Heath et al., and by 2.8× to 5.8× compared to NanoGRAM by Park et al."
    },
